ing money  well to really understand why the fed is  even a thing we have to take a look back  at history and see how things were  before the fed the fed is actually not  the first time that the us has  experimented with the idea of a national  bank in fact the us has played with  national banking since the late 1700s at  the time the national government was  extremely weak both politically and  economically the vast majority of power  was given to state governments so that  they could better tend to local needs  while this might sound great at first  glance this made the national government  rather useless they weren't able to  achieve national goals such as paying  off war debt stabilizing the currency  and rebooting the economy so the  founding fathers eventually decided to  replace the articles of confederation  which was the original document that  governed the us with the new and  improved us constitution this gave the  national government substantially more  power and treasury secretary alexander  hamilton wanted to leverage his power to  establish a national bank he felt that a  national bank would help establish  economic stability within the us and  while he did face some opposition he  would eventually get a switch in  february of 1791 when president  washington signed the bill into law  several months later in december of 1791  the first bank of the us would open for  business at first the bank primarily  dealt with the national matters for  example they collected tax revenue  distributed funds across the us and paid  for various programs and loans something  that was off limits however was buying  u.s government bonds the founding  fathers feared that this was not only an  inflation risk but that this would cause  the public to lose trust in the national  bank this also meant that the national  bank did not set monetary policy and  they did not act as a bailout service  for dying institutions they were just a  regular old bank that happened to be  used 